capture: readiness polling
runtime: JDK 8
commit: c8f6226105
source: HTTP polling of the deployed application
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no six-service reference stack and no deployed web archive on a
  servlet container, therefore no running application to poll and no readiness
  sequence to record.  Measured on the capture host rather than assumed, and
  every figure that follows is an observation.  The hostname arkcase-ce.local
  does not resolve.  The readiness target https://arkcase-ce.local/arkcase/
  and all four other documented service URLs observed HTTP status 000, which
  is this tree's token for no HTTP response at all, with transport exit status
  6 and the diagnostic "Could not resolve host: arkcase-ce.local".  That same
  diagnostic is returned when certificate verification is disabled, which
  proves the failure is name resolution and not certificate verification, so
  the self-signed certificate recorded below never even entered the picture.
  TOMCAT_HOME, CATALINA_HOME, CATALINA_BASE and CATALINA_LOG are all unset, no
  container launcher is on PATH, and no servlet container is installed in any
  of the usual locations.  No web archive of any kind exists anywhere in the
  working tree.  This region cannot be captured without a live deployment.
  The older runtime is deliberately NOT listed among the unmet preconditions,
  because it is not one: a JDK 8 installation is present on this host and
  answers 1.8.0_492, and the sibling env/toolchain.txt records that same
  banner for the baseline side.  What is missing is the deployment and its
  backing services, not the JVM.
normalised: nothing, because no poll record was captured.  The policy is
  stated in full anyway, so that the mirror reproduces the transformation
  rather than guesses at it.  When this region is captured, the pipeline
  strips timestamps and dates, the Date response header, Set-Cookie values,
  entity tags, session identifiers, generated request and instance
  identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour of a stable token, port
  assignments, and elapsed-time and duration figures.  It preserves the
  behaviour-bearing content: the attempt ordinal of every attempt, the
  observed HTTP status code of every attempt, the target path, the readiness
  verdict, and any response-body marker that evidences readiness.  The shape
  of the sequence - how many attempts it took, and which statuses preceded
  the ready one - IS the evidence this region exists to carry, so
  over-normalising it would destroy that evidence rather than tidy it.
redacted: nothing, because no poll record was captured.  When it is captured,
  every credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder
  <REDACTED>, which is the convention this documentation tree already uses.
  Three carriers are in scope.  The documented administrator credential,
  which is never transcribed here in any form, not even as an illustration of
  what redaction removes.  An Authorization header, or a userinfo component
  inside a probed URL, either of which would appear if an authenticated probe
  were ever added to this region.  And keystore or trust-store passwords,
  which a poll that echoed its transport configuration would emit, and which
  are recorded only in the redacted forms keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and
  trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>.  Session cookie values are handled by the
  normalisation policy above rather than here, because they are stripped
  outright instead of placeholdered.
----- BEGIN CAPTURE -----
----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.
  Because the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no
  poll record was invented to fill the gap.  A fabricated baseline would make
  every behaviour-preserved claim in this migration unfalsifiable, which
  defeats the baseline-as-tie-breaker rule outright.  An explicit,
  machine-readable skip with a reason is rule-compliant; an invented capture
  is not, and is strictly worse than an honest absence.
- Why an explicit skip rather than an absent or empty file.  The governing
  rule that nothing may be silently skipped is what forces this shape: a
  recursive diff between the two capture directories has to be able to SEE
  the absence and its reason.  An empty file, or no file at all, would be
  indistinguishable from a region nobody thought about, and the reader would
  have no way to tell a measured unavailability from an oversight.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record
  an absence in both places.  The sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches
  the same verdict for the eight application flows, records that readiness
  polling observed no HTTP response at all, and carries the comparator's
  overall INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result; this region is consistent
  with that record rather than an exception to it.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that
  the producer emitted this region's content on this run.  One nuance is
  worth stating precisely, because it distinguishes this region from the
  container-log region beside it: the producer's readiness probe DID execute
  on this host, and its own raw per-attempt record, status file and summary
  note exist in the notes area of this capture directory.  What it did not
  produce is a readiness sequence or a ready verdict.  Its own record shows a
  single configured attempt and a final observed status of 000, so it
  observed unreachability, established that the precondition was unmet, and
  stopped.  That measurement is recorded in skip-reason for exactly what it
  is.  It is not a captured readiness region, it is not presented as one, and
  it was not copied between the delimiters, because an observation that the
  target does not resolve is not an observation of the application becoming
  ready.
- Governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken
  build exit zero."  Applied to this region: readiness is asserted on the
  captured HTTP status and response evidence of each attempt, never on the
  exit status of a poll command.  A poll loop that exits zero because it
  stopped early is not evidence of readiness - it is evidence only that the
  loop finished.  Two properties of the producer make that concrete rather
  than theoretical: its readiness poll deliberately does not gate the flows,
  so the run continues and exits zero whether readiness was reached or not,
  and its attempt count defaults to one so that an evidence run is
  deterministic and cannot hang.  Neither the run's exit status nor the fact
  that the loop terminated carries any readiness information at all.  Only
  the recorded statuses do.
- Region-selection rule, stated here so the migrated capture reproduces it
  without guesswork.  Archive the polling sequence and nothing else: one
  record per attempt, from the first attempt through to the first ready
  response, each record carrying the attempt ordinal, the target, and the
  observed HTTP status, followed by the readiness verdict.  The window opens
  at the first attempt, which is taken before any application flow is
  attempted, and closes at the first ready response or at the exhaustion of
  the tolerated window, whichever comes first.  Every intermediate non-ready
  response inside that window belongs to the region and is recorded in place;
  none is dropped for looking untidy, and the sequence is never reordered,
  compacted or deduplicated.
- Ordering, which is what makes the eight sibling flow captures meaningful.
  The contract is that no smoke flow is attempted before ready state is
  reached: the readiness poll is the first thing directed at the application,
  it completes before any flow begins, and no flow capture in this directory
  precedes it.  The observed deviation on this host is disclosed rather than
  glossed over, because a contract stated without its exception is not a
  record.  Ready state was never reached here, and the producer deliberately
  does not gate the flows on readiness - a capture that stopped before the
  eighth flow would be a hole in the comparison rather than a baseline - so
  the eight flows still ran after the poll and each recorded its own explicit
  SKIPPED verdict naming the endpoint it could not reach.  The ordering held;
  what was absent was the ready state, not the sequence.  None of the eight
  was silently omitted, and none of them is a pass.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers the readiness poll only.  Container and
  application startup belongs to the sibling catalina.out.log in this same
  directory; toolchain provenance to env/toolchain.txt; frontend artifact
  digests to artifacts/; archived unit-test reports to surefire/; and the
  eight application flows to their own three-file captures.  The producer's
  raw per-attempt readiness output, its per-attempt status file and its
  readiness summary note live in the notes area of this capture directory:
  this file is the archived, region-scoped record under startup/, and it
  cross-references that raw output rather than reproducing it, so the poll is
  described once in each place and counted once overall.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather
  than cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare,
  leading-slash-free patterns, and those match at any depth: target and bin
  at .gitignore:6 and .gitignore:7, partition-descriptor at .gitignore:24,
  activemq-data at .gitignore:25 and logs at .gitignore:26, together with
  builds/ at .gitignore:40.  Each was verified at this file's exact depth
  with a .log file inside the candidate directory, and each was reported
  ignored; this path was reported not ignored.  The ignored token is always
  the DIRECTORY name and never the .log file extension, so a .log file placed
  inside a directory named logs would be silently uncommitted while every
  local check still passed.  This file therefore sits directly in startup/
  and no subdirectory was created beneath it.
- The same pointed irony the container-log region records applies here in
  reverse.  The readiness poll reads nothing from disk, but the failure
  criterion a reader applies to it is documented alongside a log path that
  lives in a logs directory.  Reading FROM such a directory is fine; writing
  INTO one is the trap.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs
  discovered during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one
  blocks a validation item."  Applied to this region that means every
  observed response is recorded exactly as observed: nothing repaired,
  nothing retried into submission, no non-ready response suppressed and no
  sequence cleaned up.  Raising the attempt count to cover the documented
  window is tolerance of a slow start, not a retry of a failure, and the two
  must not be confused - a poll may wait, but it may not re-run an attempt
  until it produces a better answer.  The migration's escape clause is
  invoked exactly twice in the entire change set and neither invocation is in
  this folder.  Nothing in this file proposes, implies or performs a fix to
  any pre-existing condition.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, verbatim: "The application's observed behavior
  at the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each
  resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase
  this documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution changes no
  meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  That rule is the whole reason this file exists before any migrated
  counterpart does, and the reason an absence is recorded instead of a
  plausible-looking substitute: the readiness behaviour of an application
  that was never deployed is unknowable rather than assumable.
- Provenance of the rules quoted above, and both facts belong together
  because either alone misleads.  First, the project's rules facility
  reports, verbatim, "No user rules provided" - there is no on-disk user
  rules document for this project, and that is explicitly not licence to
  lower the bar.  Second, rules are nonetheless present and binding: the
  requirements embed an explicit, numbered block of seven rules that governs
  this work in full exactly as an external rules document would, alongside
  seven transformation rules.  The identifiers the migration plan uses for
  them, R-1 through R-7 and R-T1 through R-T7, are its own navigational
  convention rather than quoted titles, which is why every rule above is
  quoted by its text rather than cited by number alone.  Mapped to those
  identifiers, the rules engaged by this file are R-5 on not disabling or
  silently skipping, R-6 on documenting rather than fixing, R-7 on baseline
  behaviour as the tie-breaker, R-T7 on evidence over exit codes, R-1 as a
  scope fence, R-2 on JDK-internal access, and R-T1 on namespace invariance.
- R-1 as a scope fence, and how it was honoured.  A change without a reason
  is out of scope, so nothing was installed to produce this file: the
  measurements in skip-reason were taken with the POSIX toolset the producer
  itself is limited to, and no field or note exists here beyond the shared
  schema, the measured skip reason, the transformation policies and the
  expectations labelled as such.  No sibling region's territory is restated,
  and no file outside this one was modified.
- R-T1, namespace invariance.  Nothing in this file references the successor
  namespace, a newer framework generation, a typed JavaScript dialect or a
  module bundler, and nothing would if this region were captured - the
  normalisation policy above preserves statuses and ordinals, not library
  identities.  The pre-migration package manager is named nowhere here, and
  nothing in this file presents it as a prerequisite or a recommendation.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy
  invented here.  At the base commit docs/setup.md:78 states that real
  keystore and trust-store passwords must not be checked into source control
  and should instead be supplied through environment variables or a secret
  manager in any non-local environment, and that page already renders both
  values as <REDACTED> at :86 and :87, and the administrator password as
  <REDACTED> at :119, where README.md does not.  This capture honours that
  posture and reuses that same token.  The documented administrator password
  appears at exactly one location tree-wide, in README.md, and it is not
  transcribed anywhere in this directory.  Two further precautions apply to
  this region specifically: the producer refuses to start without a
  credential supplied through its environment and records only that
  credential's source, never its value; and no command-trace or shell-echo
  region is enabled anywhere near a credential, because a trace of the poll
  command would defeat every redaction above.
- Verified at the base commit and after migration: the production launch
  configuration contains zero module-access JVM arguments.  If such an
  argument ever appeared in a captured poll record - for instance in a
  response body that echoed the server's launch arguments - it would be
  replaced, in this region, by the description "a module-access JVM argument
  that opens or exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed
  module", and the substitution would be recorded in this notes block.  No
  such substitution was needed, because nothing was captured.  The register
  of applied exceptions is referred to here by title only, as the JDK access
  exceptions register, because its filename embeds a token this tree
  excludes; it is delivered empty with a positive statement that none are
  required.
- Registered observation, not a defect report against this file and not
  something to fix: the current working tree has moved past the base commit,
  so every anchor cited here is cited as a base-commit anchor and should be
  re-read at commit c8f6226105 rather than at the current tip.  README.md and
  docs/setup.md are both in the migration's own scope, and the reflow of the
  duplicated launch-configuration block moved the surrounding prose, so the
  line numbers of the same statements differ in the current tree.  Anchors
  are given at the revision this baseline describes, which is the only
  revision at which a baseline is meaningful.
- Registered observation, likewise not fixed: the two recursive scans for
  module-access tokens that this deliverable is checked against cannot return
  zero across the whole documentation tree, because the register they refer
  to carries such a token inside its own required filename and other
  documents refer to it by that name.  Measured rather than estimated: five
  occurrences across four files - two migration registers, the README and the
  setup page - and every one of the five is part of that filename rather than
  a launch-configuration argument, while the register's own body contains
  none.  Those four files are outside this file's scope and were not
  modified.  Within docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is zero, and this
  file keeps it zero.
- Format note, recorded because it is a constraint rather than a preference:
  this is a plain-text capture and not a documentation page.  It carries no
  markup, no fenced block, no pipe table and no link syntax, it uses only
  line-feed line endings and no trailing whitespace, and it appears nowhere
  in the site navigation, which lists only the migration registers.  A
  documentation build therefore has nothing to warn about in this folder.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled is an EXPECTATION derived by
  reading the repository at commit c8f6226105, never an observation of a run.
  Nothing below was witnessed on this host, and every anchor below should be
  re-read at that commit rather than at the current tip.
- expected-evidence: the tolerated window is 5 to 10 minutes, documented in
  two independent places - README.md:141 states that the first startup will
  take 5 - 10 minutes, and docs/setup.md:108 repeats that the first startup
  takes 5 to 10 minutes.  The poll must tolerate the WHOLE of that window.  A
  poll that gives up inside it produces a false negative, and a readiness
  record shorter than the window is presumptively truncated rather than
  quick.  Concretely, at the producer's ten-second default interval the
  attempt count has to span at least six hundred seconds, which is sixty
  attempts; the default of one attempt exists so that an evidence run is
  deterministic and cannot hang, and it must be raised when driving a stack
  that is still starting.
- expected-evidence: the sharpest consequence of giving up early is not a
  missing readiness record, which would at least be visible.  It is that a
  sibling region drawn from the same first-startup window would report a
  process-definition count that is an artefact of timing rather than of
  behaviour, because the workflow engine deploys its definitions during that
  window.  A truncated poll therefore corrupts another region's counts while
  looking perfectly healthy itself, and a count taken against a
  half-initialised engine would be compared against a full one on the other
  side as though the difference meant something.
- expected-evidence: the target is https://arkcase-ce.local/arkcase, per
  README.md:147, which records that once the container has started
  successfully that URL should open; the producer polls that base with the
  application shell path appended.  The readiness criterion is that the
  application responds at that URL.  The failure criterion is recorded at
  README.md:143 - after the container has started, a 404 from that URL means
  startup failed - and docs/setup.md:108 states the same criterion.  A
  container that has started is not the same thing as an application that has
  started, which is why the readiness verdict is asserted on the observed
  status of the application's own URL and never on the container being up.
- expected-evidence: ArkCase serves a self-signed TLS certificate, per
  README.md:45, which records that the browser warning about the
  unrecognized, self-signed certificate has to be accepted.  A transport
  trust accommodation is therefore a correctness requirement rather than
  laziness: without one, every poll fails certificate verification before it
  ever reaches the application, and the gate yields a false negative that
  looks exactly like a broken deployment.  The producer implements that
  accommodation in a guarded form, and the guard matters as much as the
  accommodation: verification against a supplied certificate authority bundle
  is preferred, a public-key pin authenticates a self-signed peer without
  one, and the unverified-transport option is refused outright for any
  non-loopback target, because disabling verification while sending an
  administrator credential would let any host on the path present its own
  certificate and read that credential.  On this host the question was moot
  and was measured to be moot: with verification disabled the diagnostic was
  still a name-resolution failure.
- expected-evidence: attempt ordinals and observed statuses are preserved
  rather than normalised, and this is the one place where the normalisation
  policy has to be read strictly.  The clock is stripped; the sequence is
  not.  A region that recorded only its final verdict, or that collapsed
  repeated non-ready statuses into a count, would satisfy every format check
  in this tree and still have discarded the behaviour it was written to
  preserve.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is migrated/startup/readiness-poll.log:
  the same filename, the same field set in the same order, and the same
  region-selection rule stated above, so that a recursive diff between the
  two capture directories is mechanically meaningful.  A field added on one
  side only, a region selected differently on one side only, or an ordinal
  scheme that started at zero on one side would each register as a difference
  that carries no behavioural signal at all.  No wall-clock time, host name,
  user name, process identifier or absolute path is recorded anywhere above,
  for the same reason: each would differ between two runs for reasons
  unrelated to the runtime and would turn that comparison into noise.
